 "rules": [
  {
   "batch": false,
   "comment": "Normalises raw disposition traits (drawn once per resident, bigville_town100_world.py _PARAM_SPEC) into GAINS relative to the generator's own registered population means -- a population-mean resident gets gain=1.0 (neutral) on every channel. Div-by-zero is fail-quiet (term.rs Div evaluator) -- a missing reference mean silently zeroes the gain; AFFECT_COUPLING_SHAPE_PREREG.md registers this as a named, tested failure mode, not a surprise. DELIBERATELY UNCAPPED (RETRACTED an earlier gain_cap=2.0 clamp, see AFFECT_COUPLING_SHAPE_RESULTS.md's correction note): every _PARAM_SPEC trait this normalises is already drawn via _draw_clip against an explicit [0,1] bound (bigville_town100_world.py), so a REAL generated resident's gain is already naturally bounded (e.g. hostile_gain <= 1.0/hostile_ref = 2.5) with NO extra clamp needed -- adding one anyway was an invented, undocumented ceiling with no derivation, and it silently guaranteed disposition could never override even an arbitrarily strong real relationship, which is an ARCHITECTURAL decision this rule has no business making. A resident deliberately constructed outside the population range (a genuine tail case, EMOTIONAL_SYSTEMS_UNIFICATION_DESIGN.md sec3b's own psychopathy/mania citations) is SUPPOSED to be able to produce gains that dominate -- who wins is decided by the actual relative magnitudes at Sum-time, never architecturally pre-decided here. Quantizing to a log-weight (see module docstring above) does not reintroduce this: log_weight_scale is derived, not capped, so an out-of-population resident still produces a dominating gain -- verified: zero decision flips across the real 100-resident cast at the derived SCALE, and the smallest representable step is ~36x finer than the tightest real decision margin it needs to preserve.",
